#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "0350faac-e5c6-56dc-af12-3c90e0c16bf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5378" :question/problem "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x,$ find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/fd2618e961c15a9b0665e73e18544ab08b0d611b962bc3771558c61450cf49e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0).$ From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm\\dfrac57x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{b}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "961ff66e-ce82-5ef7-bd5e-d5aa14c1ec58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5379" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc71252-9b97-560d-be87-74b36d515823" :question/math-academy-id "e-7883" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{49} = 7$ and $b =\\sqrt9= 3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "5c66fba2-b645-543a-8417-bbc246db55e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7896" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6e12d114aedfd6c5c8ab5830e052fcc1dd3c937b825665a0490b42ca0c87972c.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/730325137cfca58eb17d65103e539d084f81d7ab810628261125c29dbb8a0284.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45ea980-dd72-50a4-9c54-757af2cbc3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331396} {:answer/id #uuid "37338cf5-43ef-5a88-9aaa-ef87b102e514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331397} {:answer/id #uuid "a3eda697-bd17-509e-a542-897de33720bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331398} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bab88-fbaa-5b0c-ab00-ae659f5ba695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331399} {:answer/id #uuid "400c700e-61ee-5fd9-8dcd-94bbe6c6dff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bab88-fbaa-5b0c-ab00-ae659f5ba695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331399} :answer-field/id #uuid "560064db-80ec-5712-b706-53edcb4fdb4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad96cb18-bd6b-5a64-9cd7-5ecc565723d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106522" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7420e16-5924-53f3-b61c-97b9fa39437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331403} {:answer/id #uuid "33639763-4461-572a-9af6-cdc1c81a16f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331404} {:answer/id #uuid "a375ba33-56ae-5c6a-a380-792e5d9307e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331405} {:answer/id #uuid "a59e4288-447a-5b62-8bad-e3c7b88f126f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331406} {:answer/id #uuid "53348bd7-b394-5515-8130-6324c1c9ac6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33639763-4461-572a-9af6-cdc1c81a16f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331404} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc4ac55b-74bb-5316-90a4-762217d978c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c1e05d1-cdc3-5f99-b424-96705dd43abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-106527" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85a1ee1f-2f6d-5b6a-9644-ee729c86d8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331410} {:answer/id #uuid "50179e5b-61b5-5441-9de6-6c6babadf77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{3}}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331411} {:answer/id #uuid "ec576947-6dcd-5540-98a9-843867f605d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331412} {:answer/id #uuid "f83941c5-4a0e-5a65-b35f-9bdcc282a3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331413} {:answer/id #uuid "66aec029-a516-5334-bac0-2b7aa90f0252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec576947-6dcd-5540-98a9-843867f605d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331412} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec1bf744-0b60-5665-b1c2-cea37072236d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f30b1e07-07d4-58c1-a237-16af51b64840" :question/math-academy-id "q-106528" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5)^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{12} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deb32eb7-6f5b-5ebc-8b65-ceac2de901a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331417} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7db0c5-d87c-5d5d-b02a-fe445ea7b111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331418} {:answer/id #uuid "2cec6025-ee6b-5553-a4f8-42208c9876e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331419} {:answer/id #uuid "bba4aa22-5c79-5a4a-b0e3-5a6ec79fc446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331420} {:answer/id #uuid "2e98d184-022b-5ffd-b688-2726554e9599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e98d184-022b-5ffd-b688-2726554e9599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331421} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c567a0-a6f7-5dde-b7fa-9a31eb2791a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00f90cad-f323-5203-8ecf-124e55c6a024" :question/math-academy-id "q-106598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/b876f823db1cc3be83a0ae415d0101b540527e83c818d288aa9a812d70740a99.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/d8f735790daf267a094ecd8c336d90de65d84e3985485213eb541364bd14aacd.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df4c4eb8-96a0-545d-a6f8-499124fc191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df4c4eb8-96a0-545d-a6f8-499124fc191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331424} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6875cb5-cc3c-5f78-88bf-d097db699f0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec90d138-f991-5591-a271-d92e9a9b83bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec90d138-f991-5591-a271-d92e9a9b83bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331426} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f077401-361a-57c7-a4a2-8417e7e4c1f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a09d68de-1a08-55b5-aa08-f2bd33065296" :question/math-academy-id "q-274724" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/060fcf34b388e42aa557ac8182c724236cf8ec161e37fa4fe1e96a0b63fab44f.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(2,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=2.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1d55664-2fec-560d-86a0-d2ffb3f8e6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331429} {:answer/id #uuid "027dc694-71fd-5576-965e-8fe897d3290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027dc694-71fd-5576-965e-8fe897d3290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331430} :answer-field/id #uuid "3690e7d4-c802-5423-adbb-c82a4e70da0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4c35d-5135-5e1b-ac97-3ed9b9d66581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a4c35d-5135-5e1b-ac97-3ed9b9d66581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331432} :answer-field/id #uuid "048ffd33-9d6e-5465-91ca-34315c778af1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24d693f3-c81f-529f-ae93-8dafeedac4b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-274725" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/f81019b6a22b93d480be339eb2b58c642a2a01a098d0a74eb172c873ca254b09.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(\\sqrt{5},0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925bd86f-928a-57dc-ba68-f88f11652761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331435} {:answer/id #uuid "cec9824e-1ec2-585d-9b59-5e4f1f66af43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cec9824e-1ec2-585d-9b59-5e4f1f66af43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331436} :answer-field/id #uuid "626f47da-dee6-5479-9d9b-d7ace19ed4d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62c7f0cb-17e5-59cd-badd-fe968ceaf09b" :question/math-academy-id "q-325896" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,$\n$y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{144}=12$ and $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4c4efd4-8696-5898-a4b3-798bdcb218e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c4efd4-8696-5898-a4b3-798bdcb218e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331439} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e7f442-f2af-5edb-81ed-98e3bbc10be6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6736c64-b952-51ed-a517-798f758cbddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-325899" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=64$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{64} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c3c6d74-b01d-5edf-b002-4b32b47fe7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3c6d74-b01d-5edf-b002-4b32b47fe7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442} :answer-field/id #uuid "39482ac3-275e-5c64-9ecd-bfd6084b5347" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c609c76a-8513-596f-b37c-531435907db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325901" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}=16$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{8{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41792c53-a5ce-5501-963d-7e9ab42fa018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331445} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ba4f1f-8f87-57e1-92fc-b596f0931296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7ba4f1f-8f87-57e1-92fc-b596f0931296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331446} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d330876-137e-5d67-a7ce-64e3a69c9ecc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294fa4e1-04ca-58d5-a391-b295fa7e7422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "294fa4e1-04ca-58d5-a391-b295fa7e7422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331448} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f6d8b2-3b3d-5a1c-8149-e242b9ecce0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74e4d48e-d6f1-5008-97f3-88b68b096ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325916" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/4561dbe8b730fa83f3044f7ab92bb5b2232e99b587269dbb31ffa463fd6ab348.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(1,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{b}{1} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=1\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632913a9-9187-5044-8d6c-729a81a5b2ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632913a9-9187-5044-8d6c-729a81a5b2ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d173244-2112-5a3d-9f87-a2db1e24abff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5e49199-0569-530d-8d96-4347ddf0e98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5e49199-0569-530d-8d96-4347ddf0e98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331453} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d90fa3-d556-5625-bfff-78d2a16e0840" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf58bc6d-9824-540a-8349-914446bc1cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325926" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/bf1ba7913469cf12969f8c0fa546f46b881bd38794c1b7b3d2f7500859d6ad56.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(3,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9628d248-208e-56c9-944f-e9eac8f9c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9628d248-208e-56c9-944f-e9eac8f9c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331456} :answer-field/id #uuid "759de39f-b2cd-59e7-af40-484bd1d8006d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5ac3534-c327-5568-b66f-a3a33718964d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac3534-c327-5568-b66f-a3a33718964d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331458} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f88f637-a2e4-5bdc-9efe-f14f5d46151e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a90e399-12c3-5736-af8a-cd387ebebbf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325927" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6a4ee1fd218118096dcdb2bd85b16477e2230b4634eb6c53e9fcab68f7d4939c.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(\\sqrt{6},0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{6}} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9ab8ae3-9848-5917-8d28-d29830e6a2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331461} {:answer/id #uuid "70797d34-ae52-5261-93b8-79b4498cae51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{25}-\\frac{y^2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70797d34-ae52-5261-93b8-79b4498cae51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{25}-\\frac{y^2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331462} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd1c043d-3a53-5737-ac6d-684eabdef5a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54771605-b35b-5b36-a1fc-58560ab4c2be" :question/math-academy-id "q-325936" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{8}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "563e7681-eafd-59b2-b1b9-513a98c626ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "563e7681-eafd-59b2-b1b9-513a98c626ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331465} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b4b542-af1d-5cc7-85e2-6ab75ca64c5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03028d13-f6be-53df-a555-e7beb1902b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03028d13-f6be-53df-a555-e7beb1902b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8ec832-37bc-577e-9e69-f48d738165cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be21d34c-db80-56c5-9a5f-bc12df126451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be21d34c-db80-56c5-9a5f-bc12df126451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331469} :answer-field/id #uuid "6397658f-1104-58ee-b99c-c074bea54a31" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69e36e58-af14-55f7-8286-96236a606bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-325980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/7db943c53e70d9b77490c2d73acac939be08fdfa4b0aa958addb2d50b6078543.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/e03130cdcae3fff94036453883eb638371b62a4ee26b9b550192aed32f95a39d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }\\cup \\text{ }(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e79ab75-6bf0-51df-b198-33b5f76831cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} {:answer/id #uuid "14140dcb-a619-5447-bdc3-acc8ad253aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e79ab75-6bf0-51df-b198-33b5f76831cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb3c842-a366-5fa5-b0eb-b3e573c27efb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87825b4c-9345-5473-8ba0-aee302e06a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87825b4c-9345-5473-8ba0-aee302e06a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331475} :answer-field/id #uuid "173700aa-587b-5075-ba28-7abe8c0039d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f76d65f-5129-5d16-94bc-f52d8a4e4d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325985" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire parabola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6ac31692404208d80637291fb30be028a7efdc3a172af0337da26039f1e87326.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15db1e5-1756-5827-a435-bee61be311d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331478} {:answer/id #uuid "df5063b5-4d45-5076-8a27-6a94a970cb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331479} {:answer/id #uuid "93eea5f3-1f50-5275-93d2-9129efdf0d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331480} {:answer/id #uuid "4acc9fbf-4b07-51ad-b61e-fd254420b2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331481} {:answer/id #uuid "28df5361-294d-56eb-9a12-54f0baba527d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df5063b5-4d45-5076-8a27-6a94a970cb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331479} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6291c3-8bfa-53c8-b19f-148cca1c0c2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be3bee13-1da7-5350-b9bd-f380fd194d20" :question/math-academy-id "q-7990" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-49{y}^{2}=98?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-49{y}^{2} & =98 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{98}-\\frac{49{y}^{2}}{98} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we $a=\\sqrt{49}=7$ and $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07337bbe-a6bb-5ace-80a7-21e1a98bcdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331485} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb2c1a3-7266-5620-aa10-6cb20b7b045c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331486} {:answer/id #uuid "c3651930-a7e2-5bea-bf8e-1d281958cdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331487} {:answer/id #uuid "003f5fa5-54a0-5e52-85b2-21695eb31855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331488} {:answer/id #uuid "26febcc8-7fbc-5fd6-9934-7e82e694267d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26febcc8-7fbc-5fd6-9934-7e82e694267d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331489} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8cd37a-8d2f-554e-b350-179c82e2f085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f81ef912-31de-5809-a190-3e000703e934" :question/math-academy-id "q-89197" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-36{y}^{2}=36?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-36{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{36{y}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff123e67-4836-5a51-8902-5a2f376107ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331492} {:answer/id #uuid "717013dd-2e30-5602-a1e4-797b1289cb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331493} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ccd8ba-31cd-56e6-b27d-8de7b2e23443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331494} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3f4456-7a6c-5fd2-875b-a21429c5ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331495} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ce5210-898a-581d-8c45-d553642dfd22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3ce5210-898a-581d-8c45-d553642dfd22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331496} :answer-field/id #uuid "a322e125-fa3a-561d-98ec-8055a2da81a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31514448-4195-50d5-8d70-6de439796897" :question/math-academy-id "q-89293" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d974207-11f7-5c26-ae8b-7c3ae8870574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331499} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0145f8-1c7b-57bc-bf7f-57887b782f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331500} {:answer/id #uuid "beda32a1-86fb-50fd-aea7-31a07b6249b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331501} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec629d0-52d3-505a-bcb9-b65cb5d919f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331502} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc2058-4cd0-5506-8a29-93e54a1eb58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,t\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec629d0-52d3-505a-bcb9-b65cb5d919f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331502} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9dfa021-a27c-5bbf-ad11-0eaca5b95334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f5decbaf-577e-5c3b-892a-50907a2e597c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89335" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/23610b57f6867c18f11b0b334bb4902ab92f4b111a2764aa35b5a3a1cec77e2f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/d91eb69ad250ab786426c2b46d5404bdf8aa43d6c97c1d276be444b678be4ced.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066e2feb-578b-5785-bd91-ee456cbc8c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331506} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a3d873-6d68-5ff9-9dcd-15455ae73d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331507} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e469ee-e327-55cb-8ba5-854638ecd5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\pi ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331508} {:answer/id #uuid "057fdd73-dd6b-563e-bc50-f9bcfa1485ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331509} {:answer/id #uuid "494c4a6d-3d8f-5dc1-b907-9331ef98592b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066e2feb-578b-5785-bd91-ee456cbc8c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331506} :answer-field/id #uuid "055c5eb7-77c9-58fa-a3cc-fe5b84bc4021" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29ab8af1-706d-5ba8-844f-810b6e481ba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-89350" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/50f094ee1f26d1d68672c354fc15305c18e48c6f2f97b797b45e8a228eef52b6.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5516a6bbdb411a5f21bbf530ca93926c09f9fcfdb5d57a6947032d1525849560.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}]}
